WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.460
Resource settings are security and reliability controls.

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:09.480
CPU and memory requests help Kubernetes schedule pods onto nodes with enough expected capacity.

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.820
Limits affect runtime behavior when a workload consumes more than it was allowed.

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:20.000
These settings should reflect real .NET application behavior, not guesses copied from another service.

5
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:31.400
ASP.NET Core workloads, background services, upload processing, caches, queues, parallel fan-out, and dependency calls can all create resource pressure.

6
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:44.220
Horizontal scaling can add replicas, but it does not fix unsafe per-pod behavior such as unbounded queues, unrestricted parallelism, large in-memory buffers, or work that waits indefinitely.

7
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.180
Autoscaling signals should reflect useful workload indicators.

8
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:57.640
CPU and memory are common, but queue length, request latency, dependency pressure, or custom business metrics may better describe some systems.

9
00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.540
The scaling plan should be paired with dependency capacity and application limits.

10
00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:11.620
Backpressure, request limits, queue limits, timeouts, cancellation, and rate limiting should be designed with Kubernetes resource settings.

11
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:19.160
The application should know what happens when it cannot accept more work, and callers should receive predictable responses rather than hidden buildup.

12
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:27.840
Resource pressure should be observable through metrics, logs, events, restarts, throttling, and OOM-related signals.

13
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:37.460
Developers and operators should be able to tell whether a pod is under-provisioned, over-consuming, waiting on dependencies, or retaining memory longer than intended.
